cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Who Reports Withdrawal Dates in the Western Interconnection
  Queue, and Does It Matter?
message: >-
  If you use this analysis, please cite it using the metadata
  from this file.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Parsons
    given-names: Noah
    affiliation: Independent Researcher
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0000-7224-6040'
repository-code: 'https://github.com/GuiloScion/western-queue-reporting'
abstract: >-
  Berkeley Lab's Queued Up publishes a median duration from
  interconnection request to withdrawal for the non-ISO West,
  computed from the 12.1% of western withdrawals that carry a
  recorded withdrawal date. This analysis finds that reporting
  subset compositionally unrepresentative of the region --
  technology mix, queue cohort and service type all differ at
  p < 1e-10 -- but shows that standardising to the region's
  actual composition moves the median by less than three
  months, with a sign that is not identified. Technology,
  cohort and capacity explain under 5% of the variance in
  withdrawal duration, leaving coverage rather than
  composition as the binding caveat.
keywords:
  - interconnection queue
  - electricity transmission
  - Western Interconnection
  - selection bias
  - post-stratification
  - energy policy
